'''Panmure-Ōtāhuhu''' is an electorate to the [[New Zealand House of Representatives]] in south-central [[Auckland]]. It was first contested at the {{NZ election link|2020}}, and has been held by [[Jenny Salesa]] of the [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour Party]] since its inception.

==Population centres==
It is located in south-central [[Auckland]], along the thinnest section of the [[Auckland isthmus]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.vote.nz/map/index.html?id=2&modified=2020110110227 |title=Map of electorates for the 2020 and 2023 electorates |date=17 April 2020 |access-date=18 April 2020}}</ref> The electorate consists of the mid-eastern part of the [[Manukau ward]], and a long strip of suburbs along the west bank of the Tāmaki River. The electorate divided by [[State Highway 1 (New Zealand)|State Highway 1]] and covers the titular suburbs of [[Panmure, New Zealand|Panmure]] and [[Ōtāhuhu]], as well as [[Tāmaki]], [[Point England]], [[Westfield, New Zealand|Westfield]], [[Ōtara]] and [[Papatoetoe]].<ref name="parllib">{{cite web| url= https://www.parliament.nz/en/mps-and-electorates/electorate-profiles/panmure-%C5%8Dt%C4%81huhu-electorate-profile/ |title=Panmure-Ōtāhuhu - Electorate Profile |publisher= Parliamentary Library |date= 27 April 2021 |access-date= 18 September 2025}}</ref>

==History==
Panmure-Ōtāhuhu was created in the 2019/20 redistribution, mostly from the former [[Manukau East]], but now including a large portion of the eastern part of  the [[Maungakiekie (New Zealand electorate)|Maungakiekie electorate]].<ref>{{cite news|last=Whyte |first=Anna |date=17 April 2020 |title=New electorate revealed, as raft of boundary changes announced prior to election 2020 |url=https://www.tvnz.co.nz/one-news/new-zealand/new-electorate-revealed-raft-boundary-changes-announced-prior-election-2020 |access-date=18 April 2020 |work=TVNZ}}</ref>

Rapid population growth north of Auckland resulted in a domino effect through Auckland, and in becoming Panmure-Ōtāhuhu, Manukau East was moved northward, losing a triangular area around [[Puhinui]] to {{NZ electorate link|Manurewa}} and being extended north to [[Point England]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Boundary Review 2019/20 |url=https://elections.nz/democracy-in-nz/historical-events/boundary-review-2019-2020/ |access-date=2023-11-29 |website=Elections }}</ref> Initially it was proposed to keep the name of Manukau East, but the name of Panmure-Ōtāhuhu was adopted after a public consultation period.<ref name="Commission">{{cite web|url=https://vote.nz/assets/Boundary-Review/REPORT-OF-THE-REPRESENTATION-COMMISSION-2020.pdf |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2020 |date=17 April 2020 |access-date= 18 April 2020}}</ref>

Manukau East was, since its creation in 1996, a safe [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour]] seat, held since [[2014 New Zealand general election|2014]] by [[Jenny Salesa]]. When Salesa contested the new electorate in the [[2020 New Zealand general election|2020 election]] she won again, holding the seat for Labour.<ref>{{cite web |title=Panmure-Ōtāhuhu – Official Result |url=https://www.electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2020/electorate-details-37.html |access-date=6 November 2020 |publisher=Electoral Commission}}</ref>

The electorate was disestablished for the [[2026 New Zealand general election|2026 general election]] due to population shifts in the [[Auckland]] region. The population centres of the electorate would be divided between {{NZ electorate link|Otahuhu}} and {{NZ electorate link|Maungakiekie}}.<ref>{{cite book |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2025 |url=https://elections.nz/assets/pagecomponent-file-files/REPORT-OF-THE-REPRESENTATION-COMMISSION-2025-WEB.pdf |isbn=978-0-473-75233-0 |publisher=Representation Commission |access-date=18 September 2025 |page=19 |date=8 August 2025 }}</ref>

